UPDATE tools SET description = $x$Kiro is AWS's agentic development platform built around spec-driven development, producing structured requirements, design and task specs before writing code. It now spans an IDE (a VS Code fork), a CLI, a web app, and a mobile app, and supports multiple model providers including Anthropic, OpenAI, DeepSeek, and Qwen with an Auto mode that picks a model per task.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'kiro' AND lang = 'en';

UPDATE tools SET description_long = $x$Kiro is Amazon Web Services' agentic development platform, built around spec-driven development: before generating code, it produces a structured requirements document, a design and architecture plan, and a task spec, then implements against them. The approach is aimed at reducing the rework that free-form AI coding often creates.

Kiro launched in preview in July 2025 as a VS Code-based IDE and has since expanded into a broader platform. AWS now describes it as an agent that works inside the IDE, from a CLI, in a web browser with cloud sandboxes, from a mobile app, and inside Kiro Crew, an open-source workspace.

The agent supports multiple model providers rather than a single one: Anthropic, OpenAI, DeepSeek, and Qwen, alongside open-weight models, with an Auto mode that selects a model per task. It can execute multi-file changes, run tests, and iterate against the original spec.

Pricing runs on a credit model. A Free tier ($0 per month) includes 50 credits and access to open-weight models plus a baseline Anthropic Claude model, for teams of up to 500 users. Paid tiers are billed per user per month: Kiro Pro at $20 (1,000 credits), Kiro Pro+ at $40 (2,000 credits), Kiro Pro Max at $100 (5,000 credits), and Kiro Power at $200 (10,000 credits), all with pay-as-you-go overage at $0.04 per credit. An Enterprise tier with custom pricing adds centralized billing, SSO, usage analytics, and enterprise security controls.

The spec-first workflow adds overhead for small or quick tasks, and heavy users can find the credit model costly as usage scales. Kiro's ecosystem is smaller and newer than established competitors such as Cursor or GitHub Copilot, and the free tier's model access is more limited than the paid tiers.

Kiro suits teams that want the AI to plan before it builds, and is particularly relevant for AWS-centric development shops that want an agentic tool integrated across IDE, CLI, web, and mobile surfaces.$x$
 WHERE slug = 'kiro' AND lang = 'en';

